#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142622 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ef8389c-2909-5e02-a185-817ae3c6219b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Numerator When the Result Is a Proper Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "294b83f4-2e38-5bf4-a521-01fe8ca13fe5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13831" :question/problem "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{(3 \\times 2) + 1}{2}= \\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac {7 \\times 1}{2 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{7}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142624 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb8e94ce-081f-5ea4-ac09-de30a890f4af" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Mixed Number by a Fraction When the Result Is a Proper Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "a688fc95-6831-57c6-9fee-f3295412af05" :question/math-academy-id "e-13835" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{(2 \\times 2) + 1}{2} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 5}{3 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times 2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142626 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15c48f93-e112-5d71-bb8a-c23e022931f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Numerator When the Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/id #uuid "689fd963-ba93-5b0a-b7e4-0b8fd1dc9101" :question/math-academy-id "e-13839" :question/problem "Determine the missing digit in the following equality.\n\n$0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3 \\,\\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{(3 \\times 3) + 2}{3}= \\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{3} \\times \\dfrac{2}{5} = \\dfrac{11 \\times 2}{3 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{22}{15}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{22}{15} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} 7 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{15}\n$$\n\nHence, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{7}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9f0bba3-2b88-5e77-9359-b238179c7f3d" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Mixed Number by a Fraction When the Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/id #uuid "db3701cf-af70-508a-a158-5e38ff68246f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13843" :question/problem "Multiply $1 \\, \\dfrac{7}{8} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\dfrac{7}{8}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\dfrac{7}{8} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 8) + 7}{8} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{8} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{15}{8} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} &= \\frac{15}{3} \\cdot \\frac{2}{8} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} 1 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$"}]}
